<article class="env-lemma" id="0G8X">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0G8X">Lemma <span data-tag="0G8X">15.67.2</span>.</a> Let $R$ be a ring. Let $K \in D^-(R)$. Let $m \in \mathbf{Z}$. Then $K$ is $m$-pseudo-coherent if and only if for any filtered colimit $M = \mathop{\mathrm{colim}}\nolimits M_ i$ of $R$-modules we have $\mathop{\mathrm{colim}}\nolimits \mathop{\mathrm{Ext}}\nolimits ^ n_ R(K, M_ i) = \mathop{\mathrm{Ext}}\nolimits ^ n_ R(K, M)$ for $n < -m$ and $\mathop{\mathrm{colim}}\nolimits \mathop{\mathrm{Ext}}\nolimits ^{-m}_ R(K, M_ i) \to \mathop{\mathrm{Ext}}\nolimits ^{-m}_ R(K, M)$ is injective. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      One implication was shown in Lemma <a href="/tag/0G8W" data-tag="0G8W">15.67.1</a>. Assume for any filtered colimit $M = \mathop{\mathrm{colim}}\nolimits M_ i$ of $R$-modules we have $\mathop{\mathrm{colim}}\nolimits \mathop{\mathrm{Ext}}\nolimits ^ n_ R(K, M_ i) = \mathop{\mathrm{Ext}}\nolimits ^ n_ R(K, M)$ for $n < -m$ and $\mathop{\mathrm{colim}}\nolimits \mathop{\mathrm{Ext}}\nolimits ^{-m}_ R(K, M_ i) \to \mathop{\mathrm{Ext}}\nolimits ^{-m}_ R(K, M)$ is injective. We will show $K$ is $m$-pseudo-coherent. 
    <p>
      Let $t$ be the maximal integer such that $H^ t(K)$ is nonzero. We will use induction on $t$. If $t < m$, then $K$ is $m$-pseudo-coherent by Lemma <a href="/tag/064W" data-tag="064W">15.66.7</a>. If $t \geq m$, then since $\mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}\nolimits _ R(H^ t(K), M) = \mathop{\mathrm{Ext}}\nolimits ^{-t}_ R(K, M)$ we conclude that $\mathop{\mathrm{colim}}\nolimits \mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}\nolimits _ R(H^ t(K), M_ i) \to \mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}\nolimits _ R(H^ t(K), M)$ is injective for any filtered colimit $M = \mathop{\mathrm{colim}}\nolimits M_ i$. This implies that $H^ t(K)$ is a finite $R$-module by Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/0G8N" data-tag="0G8N">10.11.1</a>. Choose a finite free $R$-module $F$ and a surjection $F \to H^ t(K)$. We can lift this to a morphism $F[-t] \to K$ in $D(R)$ and choose a distinguished triangle 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  F[-t] \to K \to L \to F[-t + 1]  \]
</div>
    <p>
       in $D(R)$. Then $H^ i(L) = 0$ for $i \geq t$. Moreover, the long exact sequence of $\mathop{\mathrm{Ext}}\nolimits $ associated to this distinguished triangle shows that $L$ inherits the assumption we made on $K$ by a small argument we omit. By induction on $t$ we conclude that $L$ is $m$-pseudo-coherent. Hence $K$ is $m$-pseudo-coherent by Lemma <a href="/tag/064R" data-tag="064R">15.66.2</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>